  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through next Thursday)\nIssued at 324 PM EST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nThe latter half of the weekend will exhibit post frontal (FROPA) \nconditions as a dry air mass (PWATs under 1 inch) will keep the \nlocal atmosphere stabilized despite a broad mid-level and upper-\nlevel trough swinging across the region. South Florida will remain \nin the right-entrance region of the jet stream, which is favorable \nfor divergence aloft and convergence in the low levels, but forecast \nsoundings highlight a highly stable environment with the dry air \nmass dominating the local atmosphere. Therefore, expect much more \ncomfortable conditions with dew points staying in the 50s and high \ntemperatures in the upper 70s on Sunday. \n\nHeading into next week, long range model guidance has continued to \ntrend towards stronger high pressure forming across the eastern US, \nsetting up brisk easterly flow across the region that results in \nbreezier than normal conditions for the early to mid-week period. \nAdditionally, as some moisture returns as a result of a mid-level \nshortwave projected to advect across the Florida Peninsula, westerly \nflow aloft will occur in tandem with the easterly surface flow.\nThis can help cause increased low-level convergence for SE \nFlorida where this wind shift and mixing overlaps. Thus, SE \nFlorida will be the primary focus area for the remainder of the \nlong term period extending through next Friday. This is shown with\nan average of 40- 50% PoPs along the east coast metro areas each \nday between Wednesday and Friday. Additional details regarding how\nmuch rainfall is possible remains highly uncertain given that \nthis time frame is near the end of the forecast period, but we \nwill continue to assess over the next several days to get an idea \nof potential QPF ranges and any possible impacts.",
